Charter school authorizations-amendments. 25LSO-0271, 1.1
FISCAL NOTE
The fiscal impact, in the form of expenditure increases, is indeterminable, but
could be significant.
This bill removes the limit on the number of newly authorized charter schools that
can be approved by the Wyoming Charter School Authorizing Board. Pursuant to 2023
Wyoming Session Laws, Chapter 179, Section 4 and 2024 Wyoming Session Laws, Chapter
118, Section 332, the Wyoming Charter School Authorizing Board can authorize up to
four charter schools through June 30, 2028. The three charter schools previously
authorized by the State Loan and Investment Board and the one charter school
authorized by the Wyoming Charter School Authorizing Board in November 2024, count
towards the total.
The Wyoming Department of Education indicates this bill may require the Legislature
to appropriate additional funds from the Public School Foundation Program Account
(SFP) for fiscal years 2026, 2027, and 2028, to account for an incalculable but
potentially significant expenditure increase in school district Foundation Program
funding, as defined in W.S. 21-13-101(a)(v), and K-12 major maintenance or school
building and facility lease payments through the State Construction Department. The
increased school district Foundation Program funding would come in the form of
school district reimbursements, school district entitlement expenditure increases
and/or school district recapture revenue decreases to the SFP, depending on the
school districts newly authorized charter schools operate in, the number of students
enrolled in the authorized charter schools, and the timing of when new charter
schools are authorized.
